The editor's A11 (T11: why do I need this, do I have the context, why is it here now), applied to the text most likely to open RC2: the revised 10-page edition. Three fictional readers chosen to be hard to please (a student, the Gen X founder, the journalist), every passage judged keep, cut, move, explain first or shorten.

Most passages pass. Eighteen fail for two or three of them: ten insider moments with no setup (the researcher's withdrawn objection, the "@", "does not hold", studio's relayed word, the lock, "parts two and three", "the split"; each needs one clause or a cut); the "Zoom in ... thirty-page edition" pointer, the editor's own example of a failure (all three); page 2, the series page, which all three would cut first (the series framing is the editor's call, G1); the dramatised note and the glosses before or after where they are needed; Web Summit without context. All three name the 18:44 scene as the best.

Five changes in order in the summary; reader by reader in review/synthetic/brutal/edition-10/. Test instruments, a read of a draft.

